From: USA-RedDragon <USA-RedDragon@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fix(approval): a tree unpacked from a downloaded forge archive is that repo's code

A subagent downloaded a repo's tarball (github.com/o/r/archive/x.tar.gz), unpacked it into a folder and built there (clang++, cmake, make). The Code from External card had only Allow and Deny: the unpack was noted as fetched code but with no source, so no "Allow code from <source> in this chat" button was offered, and a grant could not cover later builds in the tree.

A tar, bsdtar or unzip that extracts an archive the session downloaded (or a download piped into tar) into a folder (-C, --directory, -d, or after a cd into a folder under where the command works) now records that folder with the forge repo the archive came from, the same way a clone is recorded. Only a known forge URL (GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg and the like, an archive, codeload, API tarball or release asset URL) gives a source; an archive from another host, one never downloaded, two origins in one folder, listing or creating an archive give none. The card offers that repo and the grant covers later builds in the tree and a download of the same repo, never another unpacked repo, a look-alike host or a command that also names another source.

When the review model names a source as owner/repo without a host, it counts as the forge repo the session cloned or downloaded with that owner and repo, only when exactly one such repo exists; it is never guessed onto a host.
